title: Polyfill.io
page: breaches/polyfill-io.html
as-of: 2026-11-22
promise: Nothing was hacked: the polyfill.io domain was sold in February 2024, and by June its content delivery network (CDN) was serving malicious code to every site that still had the script tag.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: 490,000+ | sites affected, per later analysis | the widely quoted 100,000 was a tool's result cap
stat: None | exploitation required | the domain and its GitHub account were sold legitimately to Funnull, a Chinese company
The payload was conditional: mobile only, evading researchers. In May 2025, the US Treasury's Office of Foreign Assets Control (OFAC) sanctioned Funnull and its administrator.
notes:
Start with the four points the page leads with: two are on the tiles, and two are in the line below them.

Later analysis put the number of affected sites above 490,000. The widely reported figure, 100,000, was PublicWWW's default result cap rather than a measurement. The page describes PublicWWW as a search tool. Keep the hedge when you say the larger number: it comes from later analysis.

Nothing was exploited: the domain changed hands legitimately. In February 2024 the polyfill.io domain and its associated GitHub account were sold to Funnull, a Chinese company.

The payload was conditional: a mobile-only redirect to scam and betting destinations, gated to evade researchers. It did not fire for desktop browsers or repeat visitors, or when it detected an admin user or web analytics on the page.

In May 2025 the US Treasury's OFAC sanctioned Funnull and its administrator. OFAC is the Office of Foreign Assets Control, which administers US sanctions.

Sansec published the finding on June 25, 2024.

== What happened, and when
layout: timeline
event: February 2024 | Domain sold to Funnull; Cloudflare and Fastly then stand up safe mirrors
event: By June 2024 | The CDN is serving malicious code to sites still carrying the tag
event: June 25, 2024 | Sansec publishes the finding
event: June 26, 2024 | Cloudflare begins rewriting polyfill.io links to its mirror
event: June 27, 2024 | Namecheap suspends the domain
event: May 2025 | OFAC sanctions Funnull and its administrator, Liu Lizhi
The payload, a mobile-only redirect to scam and betting sites, was gated to evade researchers. The page gives no date for the first malicious response. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
The page gives these dates, and the timeline shows only those.

In February 2024 the polyfill.io domain and its associated GitHub account were sold to Funnull, a Chinese company. Cloudflare and Fastly stood up safe mirrors that month, after the sale. By June, the CDN was serving malicious code to every site that still had the script tag in its HTML. A CDN, a content delivery network, is a service that hosts files such as scripts and serves them to browsers. The page says only "by June": it gives no date for the first malicious response, so do not supply one.

Sansec published the finding on June 25, 2024. On June 26, Cloudflare began rewriting polyfill.io links to its mirror on sites it proxies. Namecheap suspended the domain on June 27, two days after Sansec published.

In May 2025 the US Treasury's OFAC sanctioned Funnull and its administrator, Liu Lizhi.

The page also says Google began warning advertisers whose landing pages loaded the script, but gives no date, so that is not on the timeline. The markers are evenly spaced, and the slide says the gaps between them are not to scale.

== The 100,000 figure was a search tool's result cap
layout: compare
col: Widely reported: 100,000 sites | Circulated everywhere, repeated as a measurement | It was PublicWWW's default result cap
col: Later analysis: more than 490,000 | The page's figure for affected sites | Roughly five times larger, the page says
The page's lesson: check what a widely repeated figure counted.
notes:
Two figures circulate for how many sites were affected, and they are not the same kind of number.

The 100,000 that circulated everywhere was PublicWWW's default result cap rather than a measurement. The page calls it a search tool's result cap, repeated everywhere as a measurement.

Later analysis put the affected population above 490,000 sites, which the page calls roughly five times larger. The page does not say who made the later count. Among its primary sources it lists c/side for a complete timeline and the corrected scale.

The page draws a lesson from this: check what a widely repeated figure counted. When scoping your own exposure, query your own estate rather than inferring it from a headline. That comes back as one of the defenses.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Resource Development | Domain bought | T1583.001
step: 02 | Execution | Any JavaScript, every page | T1059.007
step: 03 | Stealth | Mobile only, gated | T1497
step: 04 | Impact | Half a million sites | T1657
step: 05 | Response | Suspended, links rewritten, sanctioned | -
Arrows follow the page's stage order. Stage 05, the response, carries no ATT&CK technique.
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's five stages, each with its phase and primary ATT&CK technique.

Stage 01, Resource Development, is the sale of the domain and its GitHub account to Funnull. Stage 02, Execution, is the new owner serving any JavaScript into every page that loaded the script. Stage 03, Stealth, is the conditional delivery: mobile devices only, with checks to avoid repeat visitors, admin users and pages carrying web analytics. Stage 04, Impact, is the scale: reported as 100,000 sites, above 490,000 by later analysis, plus further CDN domains researchers linked to the same operator. Stage 05, Response, carries no ATT&CK technique: the suspension, Cloudflare's link rewriting and, in May 2025, the sanctions.

The page stresses that there is no intrusion in this chain. Someone bought a domain. The next slides take each stage in turn.

== February 2024: the domain and GitHub account were sold to Funnull
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Resource Development
technique: T1583.001 | Acquire Infrastructure: Domains
fact: What transferred | control of a script tag on hundreds of thousands of sites
fact: Exploitation | none required: a purchase
fact: Why tags persisted | added years earlier, kept in templates long past usefulness
fact: Author's view | no site in 2024 needed the polyfills at all
The buyer inherited the tags' accumulated trust without compromising anything.
notes:
In February 2024 the polyfill.io domain and its GitHub account were sold to Funnull, a Chinese company. There is no intrusion in this chain, and the page says that is what makes it worth documenting. Someone bought a domain. The page maps this stage to T1583.001, Acquire Infrastructure: Domains.

The polyfill service shimmed modern JavaScript features for older browsers. A polyfill is code that supplies a newer feature to a browser that lacks it. Developers added the tag and moved on, and the tag stayed in templates and themes for years after the browsers it supported had disappeared.

So whoever bought the domain inherited the accumulated trust of every one of those decisions, without needing to compromise anything.

Andrew Betts, who originally created the service but had never owned the domain, was blunt when it was sold: no website in 2024 needed any of the polyfills the library provided, and sites should simply remove it.

The next slide explains, in general terms, what ATT&CK means by acquiring domains.

== What ATT&CK means by acquiring domains
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1583.001 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1583/001/
ATT&CK says adversaries may acquire domains that can be used during targeting. Domains can be legitimately purchased or, in some cases, acquired for free. ATT&CK files this under Resource Development. The page says polyfill.io was sold.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK files Acquire Infrastructure: Domains under Resource Development. Adversaries may acquire domains that can be used during targeting. Domain names are the human-readable names that represent one or more IP addresses, and they can be purchased or, in some cases, acquired for free.

ATT&CK notes that domain registrars each keep a publicly viewable database of contact information for registered domains, and that private WHOIS services display other information, such as their own company data, rather than the domain's owner. WHOIS is the lookup for that registration information.

These are general points. The page says the polyfill.io domain and its GitHub account were sold to Funnull, and it does not say what the domain's registration records showed.

== The new owner could serve any JavaScript into every page
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Execution
technique: T1059.007 | Command and Scripting Interpreter: JavaScript
technique: T1195.002 | Supply Chain Compromise: Compromise Software Supply Chain
fact: Runs in | the host page's origin, with its full privileges
fact: Can | modify the DOM, read non-HttpOnly cookies, intercept forms
fact: Unaffected by | the site's TLS, patching, server hardening, dependency scanning
Script inclusion deserves the scrutiny normally reserved for dependencies.
notes:
Because the script was loaded directly into each page, the new owner could serve any JavaScript, executing with the full privileges of the host site. The page maps this stage to T1059.007, JavaScript, and T1195.002, Compromise Software Supply Chain.

A third-party script tag executes in the host page's origin. That means it can read and modify the DOM, read cookies not marked HttpOnly, intercept form input including passwords and payment details, and issue authenticated requests as the logged-in user. The DOM, the Document Object Model, is the browser's live structure of the page, which scripts can read and change. TLS, Transport Layer Security, is the encryption behind HTTPS.

The site's own security posture is irrelevant to this, the page says: TLS was intact, no server was breached, no dependency was installed. The response came fresh from a domain the site had told browsers to trust, on every page load, and the site operator had no visibility into what those bytes contained on any given request.

The page calls this the same structural exposure that makes Magecart-style card skimming work, injected script capturing payment card details, and says it is why script inclusion deserves the scrutiny normally reserved for dependencies. The next slide explains origins and HttpOnly cookies in general.

== What an origin is, and what HttpOnly hides
layout: background
source: RFC 6454, The Web Origin Concept | https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6454
credit: Also from the OWASP Session Management Cheat Sheet (cheatsheetseries.owasp.org)
An origin is a URL's scheme, host and port. RFC 6454 says a page may run script from any origin, and scripts imported into a document can access every resource in its origin. Browsers keep HttpOnly cookies from scripts.
notes:
Background, from the RFC on the web origin concept and from OWASP, in general terms.

RFC 6454, The Web Origin Concept, says browsers group URIs into protection domains called origins. Two URIs share an origin if they have the same scheme, host and port, so http://example.com and https://example.com are different origins.

Not every resource carries the same authority. An image carries none, but an HTML document carries the full authority of its origin, and scripts within or imported into the document can access every resource in its origin. Reading information from another origin is generally forbidden, yet an origin is permitted to execute script from any origin.

OWASP's Session Management Cheat Sheet describes the HttpOnly cookie attribute: it instructs web browsers not to let scripts access the cookie through the DOM's document.cookie object. OWASP adds that HttpOnly protects only the cookie's confidentiality, because the browser still includes cookies in the requests it sends.

Also from the OWASP Session Management Cheat Sheet: https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Session_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html

== Mobile only, suppressed for repeat visitors, admin users and analytics pages
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Stealth
technique: T1497 | Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion
technique: T1027 | Obfuscated Files or Information
fact: Suppressed for | repeat visitors, admin users, and pages carrying web analytics
fact: A developer saw | a clean, working polyfill script
fact: Why checking fails | the operator chooses the response per request
fact: Payload | a redirect to scam and sports-betting destinations
You cannot check what a third-party CDN serves by looking at what it serves you.
notes:
Server-side conditional delivery is what let this run for months, the page says. The malicious response was returned only to mobile browsers, and suppressed for repeat visitors, for admin users, and on pages carrying web analytics, so a developer checking the site from a desktop saw a clean, functional polyfill script. An admin user is someone signed in to manage the site; web analytics are services that measure a site's visitors.

The page maps this stage to T1497, Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion, and also lists T1027, Obfuscated Files or Information.

That defeats the obvious verification. You cannot check what a third-party CDN is serving by looking at what it serves you, because the operator decides per request.

The payload was a redirect to scam and sports-betting destinations. The next slide shows who saw what, and the one after explains, in general terms, what ATT&CK means by sandbox evasion.

== Who saw the payload, and who could have noticed
layout: compare
col: A developer checking the site | Checked it from a desktop | Saw a clean, functional polyfill script | Among those systematically excluded from seeing it
col: A mobile visitor | The malicious response went only to mobile browsers | Suppressed for repeat visitors, admin users and analytics pages | Those redirected had no idea which site sent them there
The page says the affected and observing populations barely overlapped.
notes:
The page draws out what the gating meant for who could notice.

A developer checking the site from a desktop saw a clean, functional polyfill script. The page says the people who could have noticed were systematically excluded from seeing it.

The malicious response was returned only to mobile browsers, and suppressed for repeat visitors, for admin users, and on pages carrying web analytics. The users being redirected to betting sites had no idea which site had sent them there.

So the affected population and the observing population barely overlapped. Keep the word barely when you say it.

One of the page's defenses answers this directly: monitor from the outside, in the conditions your users experience. It comes back after the defense matrix.

== What ATT&CK calls sandbox evasion
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1497 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1497/
ATT&CK describes adversaries detecting analysis environments and changing what they do when they find one: disengaging, or concealing core functions. Methods include checking for security monitoring tools or for legitimate user activity. The page calls this incident's gating server-side.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK's Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion covers adversaries detecting and avoiding virtualization and analysis environments. That may include changing behavior based on checks for artifacts of a virtual machine or sandbox. If the adversary detects one, ATT&CK says, they may make their malware disengage from the victim or conceal the core functions of the implant.

Methods ATT&CK lists include checking for security monitoring tools, such as Sysinternals or Wireshark, or for other artifacts of analysis or virtualization, and checking for legitimate user activity. Others are sleep timers or loops in malware code, to avoid running inside a temporary sandbox.

ATT&CK places the technique under the Stealth and Discovery tactics.

These are general descriptions. The page calls this incident's gating server-side conditional delivery, with the operator choosing the response per request.

== Later analysis put the affected sites above 490,000, not 100,000
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Impact
technique: T1657 | Financial Theft
fact: Widely reported | 100,000 sites: PublicWWW's default result cap
fact: Linked domains | more CDN and library domains linked to the same operator
fact: Harm delivered | redirects to scam and betting sites, aimed at end users
fact: Harm available | anything JavaScript can do in the host origin
Direct harm was mostly to end users, but the access available was much broader.
notes:
The 100,000 number that circulated everywhere was PublicWWW's default result cap rather than a measurement, and later analysis put the affected population above 490,000 sites. The page maps this stage to T1657, Financial Theft.

Researchers also linked the same operator to further CDN and library domains. The page says those domains served a much larger population and extended the potential reach considerably further. It gives no number for that population, so do not put one on it.

Because the payload was a redirect to scam and betting sites rather than a card skimmer, the direct harm was mostly to end users rather than site operators. The access available was much broader than what was used: anything JavaScript can do in the host origin, as stage 02 described.

== Namecheap suspended the domain; Cloudflare began rewriting links to its mirror
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Response
fact: June 26, 2024 | Cloudflare begins rewriting polyfill.io links to its mirror
fact: June 27, 2024 | Namecheap suspends the domain
fact: Safe mirrors | Cloudflare and Fastly, since February 2024, after the sale
fact: May 2025 | OFAC sanctions Funnull and administrator Liu Lizhi
fact: Implication | most affected operators were rescued rather than remediating
Containment came from infrastructure providers rather than the affected sites.
notes:
Stage 05 is the response, and the page maps it to no ATT&CK technique.

Namecheap suspended the domain on June 27, 2024, two days after Sansec published. Cloudflare and Fastly had stood up safe mirrors in February 2024, after the sale, and on June 26 Cloudflare began rewriting polyfill.io links to its mirror on sites it proxies, so operators who could not quickly edit their templates were covered. Google began warning advertisers whose landing pages loaded the script.

In May 2025 the US Treasury's OFAC sanctioned Funnull and its administrator, Liu Lizhi.

Containment came from infrastructure providers rather than from the affected sites, because the affected sites mostly did not know they were affected. Keep the word mostly.

No defense on the page is tied to this stage. That gap is one of the discussion questions at the end.

== Who contained it, and who mostly did not know they were affected
layout: compare
col: Infrastructure providers | Namecheap suspended the domain two days after Sansec published | Cloudflare began rewriting proxied sites' polyfill.io links to its mirror | Google warned advertisers whose landing pages loaded the script
col: The affected sites | Mostly did not know they were affected | Most operators were rescued rather than remediating | That rescue was not a control they could have relied on
Registrars and CDNs acting on their behalf saved hundreds of thousands of site owners, the page says.
notes:
The page asks you to note what this response chain implies.

On one side, infrastructure providers. Namecheap suspended the domain two days after Sansec published. Cloudflare and Fastly had stood up safe mirrors in February, after the sale, and from June 26 Cloudflare began rewriting polyfill.io links to its mirror on sites it proxies, so operators who could not quickly edit their templates were covered. Google began warning advertisers whose landing pages loaded the script.

On the other side, the affected sites. They mostly did not know they were affected, and most affected operators were rescued rather than remediating.

The page's conclusion: hundreds of thousands of site owners were saved by registrars and CDNs acting on their behalf, which is not a control any of them could have relied on.

== A script tag trusts whoever owns its domain today
layout: text
- A script src on someone else's domain is a standing grant of arbitrary code execution.
- It runs in your users' browsers, renewed on every page load.
- Whoever owns that domain today can revoke it, not whoever owned it then.
notes:
The page draws its lesson in its summary: the supply-chain lesson here is not about packages at all.

A script tag with a src pointing at someone else's domain is a standing grant of arbitrary code execution in your users' browsers. It is renewed on every page load, and it is revocable by whoever owns that domain today rather than whoever owned it when you added the tag.

Stage 02 draws the same conclusion: script inclusion deserves the scrutiny normally reserved for dependencies. The page's inventory defense repeats it too: every external origin your pages load is a standing grant of code execution in your users' browsers.

== Six defenses against five stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05
row: Self-host, or pin with Subresource Integrity | 02
row: Inventory external origins, delete the unneeded | 01
row: Content Security Policy | -
row: Treat domain ownership changes as security events | 01
row: Monitor from outside, as users experience it | 03
row: Check what a quoted number counted | 04
No defense on the page is tied to stage 05, the response.
notes:
This matrix sets the page's six defenses against its five stages. A cell is marked only where a defense's own words cite that stage's facts.

Pinning with Subresource Integrity cites the content served into the page, stage 02: the browser refuses content that does not match, which the page says would have broken this attack on day one. The inventory defense cites tags that stayed for years after the polyfills became unnecessary, stage 01. Treating ownership changes as a security event cites a change of control, the sale in stage 01. Monitoring from outside cites the mobile-only payload that desktop checks did not see, stage 03. Checking what a number counted cites the 100,000 result cap, stage 04.

The CSP defense names no single stage. It says CSP would not have stopped polyfill.io itself, since the site had allowed that origin, but that it constrains the payload's second stage and is the difference between a redirect and a full card skimmer.

Column 05 is empty. No defense cites the suspension, the mirrors, the link rewriting or the sanctions. That gap is a discussion question at the end.

== Self-host third-party scripts, or pin them with SRI
layout: compare
col: Pin with SRI (stage 02) | An SRI hash makes the browser refuse content that does not match | It would have broken this attack on day one, for every site using it | It would break the script cleanly rather than silently serve malware
col: Or self-host | SRI cannot be used with a service that intentionally varies its response | The page calls that the argument for self-hosting instead | csoh.org applies this discipline to its own assets
SRI stands for Subresource Integrity.
notes:
The page's first defense is to self-host third-party scripts, or pin them with Subresource Integrity, SRI.

An SRI hash on a script tag makes the browser refuse content that does not match. The page says that would have broken this attack on day one for every site that used it, and broken the script cleanly rather than silently serving malware.

SRI has a limit. It cannot be used with a service that intentionally varies its response, and the page says that is itself the argument for self-hosting instead.

The page adds that csoh.org applies exactly that discipline to its own assets, as described on its page about how csoh.org is secured, linked at the end of this deck.

== Inventory what your pages load, and watch who owns it
layout: compare
col: Inventory origins (stage 01) | The tags stayed for years because nobody audits script includes | Enumerate origins across templates, themes and tag manager | Confirm each has an owner and a current reason; remove the rest
col: Watch ownership (stage 01) | This attack is entirely a change of control | Watch WHOIS and registrar changes for origins you rely on | Be suspicious when a long-dormant open-source service changes hands
The same instinct applies to maintainership changes in packages, the mechanism behind XZ Utils and event-stream.
notes:
Two defenses come straight from stage 01.

First, inventory every external origin your pages load, and delete what you no longer need. The polyfills had been unnecessary for years; the tags stayed because nobody audits script includes. Enumerate third-party origins across your templates, themes and tag manager, confirm each has an owner and a current reason, and remove the rest. The page says every one is a standing grant of code execution in your users' browsers.

Second, treat domain ownership changes in your dependencies as a security event. The page calls this attack entirely a change of control. Watch WHOIS and registrar changes for the external origins you depend on, and be suspicious when a long-dormant open-source service suddenly changes hands or becomes newly energetic. WHOIS lookups return a domain's public registration details.

The page says the same instinct applies to maintainership changes in packages, the mechanism behind XZ Utils and event-stream. Both have their own kill chains, linked at the end.

== A Content Security Policy limits what an inherited domain can do
layout: text
- A strict script-src allow-list limits which origins execute.
- A connect-src policy constrains where injected code can send data.
- It would not have stopped polyfill.io itself, an allowed origin.
- It is the difference between a redirect and a full card skimmer.
notes:
The page ties this defense to no single stage, so its matrix row is unmarked.

A Content Security Policy, CSP, is a response header that tells the browser which scripts and other resources a page may load. A strict script-src allow-list limits which origins can execute at all, and a connect-src policy constrains where injected code can send data.

The page is plain about the limit. CSP would not have stopped polyfill.io itself, since the site had explicitly allowed that origin. What it does is constrain the payload's second stage, and the page says that is the difference between a redirect and a full card skimmer.

== Monitor as your users experience it, and check what numbers counted
layout: compare
col: Monitor from outside (stage 03) | The payload only fired for mobile users, so desktop checks saw nothing | Synthetic monitoring from mobile user agents would have surfaced this | So would real-user monitoring that reports unexpected navigations
col: Check the count (stage 04) | The 100,000 was a search tool's result cap, repeated as a measurement | The true figure was roughly five times larger | Scope your own exposure by querying your own estate
Testing only as a developer on a laptop tests the configuration the attacker excluded.
notes:
Two more defenses each answer one stage.

For stage 03, monitor from the outside, in the conditions your users experience. The payload only fired for mobile users, so desktop checks saw nothing. Synthetic monitoring from mobile user agents, and real-user monitoring that reports unexpected navigations, would both have surfaced this. Synthetic monitoring runs scripted checks against a site from outside; real-user monitoring reports what real visitors' browsers experience; a mobile user agent is a phone's browser, or a test that presents itself as one. If you only ever test as a developer on a laptop, the page says, you are testing the exact configuration the attacker excluded.

For stage 04, check what a widely quoted number counted. The 100,000 figure was a search tool's result cap, repeated everywhere as a measurement, and the page calls the true figure roughly five times larger. When scoping your own exposure, query your own estate rather than inferring it from a headline.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Does every third-party script carry an SRI hash, or is it self-hosted?
check: Can you list every external origin your templates, themes and tag manager load?
check: Does each of those origins have an owner and a current reason?
check: Would you notice WHOIS or registrar changes for the origins you depend on?
check: Does your Content Security Policy limit script-src and connect-src?
check: Do you monitor as a mobile user agent, and report unexpected navigations?
check: Have you sized your exposure from your own estate, not a headline?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask whether every third-party script on your pages carries an SRI hash or is self-hosted, remembering that SRI cannot be used with a service that intentionally varies its response. Ask whether you can list every external origin across your templates, themes and tag manager, and whether each has an owner and a current reason to be there.

Ask whether anyone would notice WHOIS or registrar changes for the origins you depend on. Ask whether your Content Security Policy limits script-src and connect-src.

Ask whether you monitor from mobile user agents, with real-user monitoring that reports unexpected navigations. Finally, ask whether you sized your own exposure by querying your own estate rather than from a headline.

== Would any of your checks have seen this payload?
layout: question
The payload fired only for mobile browsers, and was suppressed for repeat visitors, for admin users, and on pages carrying web analytics.

Name one check in your environment that runs in the conditions your users experience.
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one before moving on.

The page says the malicious response was returned only to mobile browsers, and suppressed for repeat visitors, for admin users, and on pages carrying web analytics, so a developer checking the site from a desktop saw a clean, functional polyfill script.

Ask each person to name one check from their own environment, and the conditions it runs in: which device, which browser, first visit or repeat, signed in as an admin or not, on a page with analytics or without. Then show the answer slide, which gives what the page says would have surfaced this.

== What the page says would have surfaced it
layout: answer
Desktop checks saw nothing, the page says.
- Synthetic monitoring from mobile user agents would have surfaced this.
- So would real-user monitoring that reports unexpected navigations.
- Testing only as a developer on a laptop tests the configuration the attacker excluded.
notes:
The page's answer is to monitor from the outside, in the conditions your users experience.

The payload only fired for mobile users, so desktop checks saw nothing. Synthetic monitoring from mobile user agents and real-user monitoring that reports unexpected navigations would both have surfaced this.

Bring the group's examples back to this. If a check only ever runs as a developer on a laptop, the page says, it tests the exact configuration the attacker excluded.

== What would you add for stage 05?
layout: question
The page says most affected operators were rescued by registrars and CDNs rather than remediating, and ties no defense to the response.

What would you add, and which reported detail would it change?
notes:
This question comes from the gap in the defense matrix.

The page offers defenses tied to stages 01, 02, 03 and 04. It ties nothing to stage 05, the response.

The page is direct about that stage. Containment came from infrastructure providers rather than from the affected sites, because the affected sites mostly did not know they were affected. Hundreds of thousands of site owners were saved by registrars and CDNs acting on their behalf, which is not a control any of them could have relied on.

Let the group propose ideas before you show the answer slide. Ask each proposal to name the detail from the page that it would have changed.

== The page ties no defense to stage 05
layout: answer
The page ties no defense to the response, and this deck adds none. Test any idea against stage 05: suspension two days after Sansec published, Cloudflare's link rewriting for operators who could not quickly edit templates, and Google's advertiser warnings.
notes:
Be clear that the page ties no defense to stage 05, and this deck adds none of its own.

Test each idea the group proposes against the facts the page reports for stage 05. Namecheap suspended the domain on June 27, 2024, two days after Sansec published. Cloudflare and Fastly had stood up safe mirrors in February 2024, and on June 26 Cloudflare began rewriting polyfill.io links to its mirror on sites it proxies, so operators who could not quickly edit their templates were covered. Google began warning advertisers whose landing pages loaded the script. In May 2025, OFAC sanctioned Funnull and its administrator.

Some proposals will be the page's own defenses, tied to other stages. Monitoring from the outside is tied to stage 03, and the inventory of external origins to stage 01. The group may argue that either reaches stage 05; that argument is the group's own.

The page's reading is that most affected operators were rescued rather than remediating. Any proposal should say how a site would have known it was affected, and who would have acted.

== Sources
layout: links
link: Sansec, the original research | https://sansec.io/research/polyfill-supply-chain-attack
link: Censys, the web of compromised domains | https://censys.com/blog/july-2-polyfill-io-supply-chain-attack-digging-into-the-web-of-compromised-domains/
